    "ogImage": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1507003211169-0a1dd7228f2d?w=1200&q=80",
    "heading": "How to change jobs: a practical guide to making the right choice",
    "subheading": "Changing jobs is one of the most important professional decisions you can make. It can represent a growth opportunity or turn into a poor choice if approached impulsively.",
    "bodyText": "Changing jobs is one of the most important professional decisions a person can make.\n\nIt can represent a growth opportunity, an improvement in working conditions or the beginning of a new career path.\n\nBut it can also turn into a poor choice if approached impulsively.\n\nMany professionals start looking for a new position after a particularly difficult day, a conflict with a manager or a period of intense stress.\n\nUnderstandable.\n\nHowever, the best decisions rarely come from an emotional reaction.\n\nBefore updating your CV or sending out applications, it is worth pausing for a moment and asking yourself some fundamental questions.\n\nIn this guide we will look at how to determine whether it is truly time to change jobs, how to evaluate a new opportunity and which mistakes to avoid.\n\nWhen is it truly time to change jobs?\n\nThere is no one-size-fits-all answer.\n\nEvery situation is different.\n\nHowever, there are certain signals that deserve attention.\n\nYou have stopped learning\n\nOne of the most common reasons that drive people to seek a new opportunity is the lack of growth.\n\nIf you have been performing the same tasks for years without acquiring new skills, it may be time to reflect on your career path.\n\nYou see no future prospects\n\nEvery professional needs to perceive a possible evolution.\n\nWhen the future appears blocked and there are no development opportunities, motivation tends to decline.\n\nThe job is no longer aligned with your goals\n\nPriorities change over time.\n\nWhat was important five years ago may no longer be relevant today.\n\nFor this reason it is perfectly normal to reassess your professional choices.\n\nThe work environment has become a problem\n\nConstant stress, ongoing conflicts or a negative company culture can have a significant impact on personal well-being.\n\nQuestions to ask yourself before starting your search\n\nMany people know what they no longer want.\n\nFar fewer know what they are actually looking for.\n\nThis is a mistake.\n\nWhat do I really want to change?\n\nUnderstanding the true reason behind the change helps to avoid impulsive choices.\n\nSalary? Responsibilities? Industry? Working hours? Leadership? Work environment?\n\nWhat are my priorities?\n\nEvery opportunity involves trade-offs.\n\nDefining your priorities allows you to evaluate offers more clearly.\n\nWhat skills can I leverage?\n\nBefore looking at the market, it is worth analysing your own professional value.\n\nWhich skills make you an attractive candidate? Which results can you demonstrate?\n\nA very common mistake: running away instead of choosing\n\nThere is an important difference between leaving a job and choosing a new opportunity.\n\nMany candidates focus exclusively on the first part. The goal becomes leaving as quickly as possible.\n\nThe risk is accepting an offer that presents problems similar to those you are trying to escape.\n\nChanging jobs does not simply mean leaving. Above all, it means choosing where to go.\n\nHow to evaluate a new opportunity\n\nDo not stop at the job title or the salary.\n\nLook at the overall picture.\n\nThe company\n\nIs it stable? Does it have a good reputation? Does it communicate transparently?\n\nThe role\n\nAre the responsibilities clear? Are there opportunities for growth? Are the expectations realistic?\n\nThe direct manager\n\nMany people change companies. Many others change managers.\n\nUnderstanding who you will work with every day is often more important than you might think.\n\nThe company culture\n\nHow are people treated? How are decisions made? Which values emerge in practice?\n\nProfessional growth\n\nWill the opportunity allow you to learn something new?\n\nIn five years, will you be a better professional thanks to this experience?\n\nHow to look for a job while still employed\n\nMany professionals search for a new opportunity while already holding a position.\n\nThis situation offers some important advantages.\n\nLess pressure\n\nWhen there is no immediate financial urgency, it is easier to evaluate opportunities with a clear head.\n\nGreater negotiating power\n\nThose who already have a job can generally afford to be more selective.\n\nHow to organise your search\n\nAn effective search does not necessarily require many hours each day. Above all, it requires method.\n\nUpdate your CV. Update LinkedIn. Define your objectives. Monitor the market.\n\nThe most common mistakes when changing jobs\n\nMany people make avoidable mistakes, often because they focus exclusively on the most immediate aspects.\n\nLooking only at the salary. Accepting too quickly. Not asking questions during the interview. Speaking negatively about your current employer. Changing without a clear direction.\n\nHow to leave your job professionally\n\nEven when you have already accepted a new opportunity, the way you leave a company matters. A great deal.\n\nCommunicate your decision clearly. Maintain professionalism until your last day. Facilitate the handover process. Do not burn bridges unnecessarily.\n\nThe job market is smaller than it seems. Relationships built over time can prove invaluable in the future.\n\n30-day practical plan\n\nIf you are thinking about changing jobs, you can start with a simple and structured approach.\n\nAnalysis: Why do I want to change? What am I looking for? What are my priorities? Preparation: Update your CV. Update LinkedIn. Update your portfolio (if relevant). Research: Monitor the market. Identify the most interesting opportunities. Action: Send targeted applications. Prepare for interviews.\n\nConclusion\n\nChanging jobs can be one of the most important decisions in your career, which is why it deserves time, reflection and preparation.\n\nThe best choices rarely come from haste.\n\nThey come from the ability to understand what you want to build in the long term.\n\nBefore sending out applications, ask yourself not only what you want to leave behind.\n\nAsk yourself above all where you want to arrive.\n\nYour next job may be closer than you think.\n\nIf you are looking for it, you will find it on JobCourier.",
